{"word": "psychologist", "source": "freedictionaryapi", "data": {"word": "psychologist", "entries": [{"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "noun", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˌsaɪˈkɑl.ə.d͡ʒɪst/", "tags": ["US"]}], "forms": [{"word": "psychologists", "tags": ["plural"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "An expert in the field of psychology.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "I don't go into this with the idea of it turning out wrong but in the conviction that it's going to be stupendous. You're a poor psychologist, Nickle, but never mind […]", "reference": "1934, Ernest Bramah, The Bravo of London:"}, {"text": "Not much later—perhaps only two or three weeks later he has discovered meaning in them; he knows that they say \"The cat sat on the hat.\" How this happens no one really knows, despite the efforts of philosophers and psychologists over two and a half millennia to study the phenomenon.", "reference": "1972, Mortimer J. Adler, Charles Van Doren, How to Read a Book, →ISBN, →LCCN, page 25:"}, {"text": "In 2009, the Obama Administration released guidelines on enhanced interrogation written in 2003 and 2004 by the CIA Office of Medical Services. ^((OMS).1-3(appendix F)) The OMS guidelines, even in redacted form, and opinions from the US Department of Justice's (DOJ’s) Office of Legal Counsel show that CIA physicians, psychologists, and other health care personnel had important roles in enhanced interrogation.", "reference": "2010 August 4, Leonard S. Rubenstein, JD; Stephen N. Xenakis, MD, “The Ethics of Enhanced Interrogations and Torture: A Reappraisal of the Argument”, in JAMA, volume 304, number 5, American Medical Association, →DOI, pages 569–570:"}, {"text": "Investigating why IBM salespeople were so successful, researchers found that IBM set easy targets for their salespeople rather than targets that were difficult to reach and so most of their salespeople achieved their targets. IBM had hired psychologists to define optimum motivational goals for their salespeople and it was discovered that if the targets were achievable the salespeople became very confident and motivated (unlike their demotivated cousins in rival companies) and went out and sold even more computers.", "reference": "2012, Christine Wilding, chapter 2, in Cognitive Behavioural Therapy, Croydon, UK: CPI Group (UK) Ltd, page 18:"}, {"text": "Self-help programs can give people the nudge they need to see a psychologist or encourage healthy changes, but that is not always the case, experts say. […] Redding’s study asked four psychologists with expertise in anxiety and depressive disorders to rate each self-help book on five criteria: […]", "reference": "2019 May 2, Nina Avramova, “When you should use self-help programs and when to skip them”, in CNN, archived from the original on 13 Jun 2025:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [{"language": {"code": "ar", "name": "Arabic"}, "word": "نَفْسَانِيّ"}, {"language": {"code": "ar", "name": "Arabic"}, "word": "عَالِم النَّفْس"}, {"language": {"code": "ar", "name": "Arabic"}, "word": "أَخِصَّائِيّ عِلْمِ النَّفْس"}, {"language": {"code": "hy", "name": "Armenian"}, "word": "հոգեբան"}, {"language": {"code": "ast", "name": "Asturian"}, "word": "sicólogu"}, {"language": {"code": "ast", "name": "Asturian"}, "word": "sicóloga"}, {"language": {"code": "be", "name": "Belarusian"}, "word": "псіхо́лаг"}, {"language": {"code": "be", "name": "Belarusian"}, "word": "псыхо́лёг"}, {"language": {"code": "bg", "name": "Bulgarian"}, "word": "психоло́г"}, {"language": {"code": "bg", "name": "Bulgarian"}, "word": "психоло́жка"}, {"language": {"code": "ca", "name": "Catalan"}, "word": "psicòleg"}, {"language": {"code": "ca", "name": "Catalan"}, "word": "psicòloga"}, {"language": {"code": "cmn", "name": "Chinese Mandarin"}, "word": "心理學家 /心理学家"}, {"language": {"code": "kw", "name": "Cornish"}, "word": "brysonydh"}, {"language": {"code": "kw", "name": "Cornish"}, "word"